  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 320 PM EDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\n- Fairly quiet weather tonight and Friday\n\nClouds will generally be on the decrease tonight with partly\ncloudy to mostly clear conditions developing by daybreak. \nRainfall and clearing skies leads to the thought of fog \ndevelopment. Winds around 1,000 feet will hold at 10-20 knots \nwhich should preclude widespread fog. We are thinking some light \nfog development is possible especially along the I-94 corridor. On\nFriday, mid and high clouds will thicken in advance of our next\nsystem. Skies will likely be mostly cloudy at sunset. We expect to\nremain dry through the day on Friday as the system will still be\nout in the Plains. Fall like temperatures tonight and Friday with\nlows in the 40s over Central Lower Michigan and highs in the \nlower to middle 70s in most areas tomorrow. \n\n- Heavy Rain expected Friday night and into the weekend\n\nThe main focus of the 7 day forecast is on Friday night into\nSaturday and potentially into the weekend as well with the threat\nof heavy rain. \n\nA shortwave will eject out of the Western U.S. trough and move\ninto the Northern Plains tonight. This shortwave will then shear \neastward into the Great Lakes region Friday into Saturday. A\nsurface boundary will remain off to our south during this time and\nprovide a lifting and focusing mechanism for the potential of\nheavy rain. An 850mb low level jet will be focused right at \nSouthwest Lower Michigan late Friday night and Saturday morning.\nThe current forecast is for 1-2 inches of rain with this lead\nevent (Friday night/Saturday morning).\n\nAdditional rain is possible Saturday night into Monday as the\nsurface boundary is still in fairly close proximity. Additional\nmid level shortwaves are forecast to ride eastward through the\nregion during this time. Additional rain of an inch or so is\npossible, but the position of the low level jet is in question.\nConvection to the south can modulate the area of heavy rain quite\na bit so confidence in the Saturday night through Monday rainfall\nis lower. \n\nWe are likely looking at some ponding of water in low spots at\ntimes of the heavier rainfall as well as rises on area rivers. At\nthis point however we are not expecting widespread river flooding.\nWe could be looking at some short term river flood advisories. We\nfeel the location and magnitude of the heavier rain will come more\ninto focus tomorrow. \n\n- Near normal temperatures through the 7 day forecast\n\nTemperatures this time of year typically are around 70 for highs\nand 50 for lows. We are not expected to be far off from that\nthrough the period. Lows will generally be in the 50s with highs\nin the upper 60s to lower 70s.",
